abstract: |
  Background: To get the most insight out of current omics analyses it is necessary to combine various (bioinformatics) software tools. To achieve this in an
  easy and reproducible way, workflow engines like Nextflow or Snakemake are used to build sophisticated data analysis pipelines. While those engines are powerful
  tools for developers, they are a high obstacle to non-technical users due to their unfamiliarity to the command line or to Unix-like operating systems.

  Results: The Massively Accessible Workflow Platform (MaCWorP) is designed to overcome these limitations by making powerful data analysis pipelines accessible
  to less technically affine users via a web interface independent of the underlying workflow engine. Users can upload their data and run the embedded workflows
  on the platform using a simple web browser. Furthermore, any result of the workflows can be visualized directly inside the browser as images, tables, interactive plots and more.

  Conclusions: MAcWorP enables developers to quickly deploy Nextflow and Snakemake workflows on a web-based platform which creates a graphical user interface for the
  workflow parameterization, execution and visualization of results. MAcWorP is open source and freely available at https://github.com/cubimedrub/macworp
